---
/**
 * Footer — the small print, and the only place it is written. Blog pages
 * get it after <main>, where its plain-text line doubles as the no-JS
 * way home. The home page carries no footer at all: the finale's contact
 * line is that page's sign-off.
 *
 * The top hairline is the one rule left on it: it seats the colophon under
 * the page proper, which is a real content break rather than decoration.
 *
 * Plain body type, not the spaced small caps the rest of the site's metadata
 * wears: this is a sentence about who made the page, and it should read like
 * one. The 64rem column is the blog's own frame, so the two ends land on the
 * same margins as the post above them.
 */
---

<footer class="hairline border-t">
  <p class="foot-print mx-auto flex flex-wrap justify-between gap-x-8 gap-y-1 px-5 py-6 text-muted sm:px-8">
    <span>© 2026 GNCE Onyx</span>
    <span>Weston High School · Team&nbsp;#37122</span>
  </p>
</footer>

<style>
  /* Ubuntu at reading weight, normal case, no tracking. */
  p.foot-print {
    max-width: 64rem;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    letter-spacing: normal;
    text-transform: none;
  }

  /* The nav toggle parks over the bottom right corner of every page, and
     this print is the last thing under it. The right end has to clear the
     toggle's square plus its inset (4.5rem at the 1rem mobile inset, 6rem
     at the 1.5rem one), and the centering margin only helps once the
     viewport outgrows the measure: the padding picks up whatever the
     margin does not cover, then falls back to the column's own padding on
     wide screens. Typed with the element to outrank the utilities. */
  p.foot-print {
    padding-inline-end: max(1.25rem, calc(4.5rem - (100vw - min(100vw, 64rem)) / 2));
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    p.foot-print {
      padding-inline-end: max(2rem, calc(6rem - (100vw - min(100vw, 64rem)) / 2));
    }
  }
</style>
